  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 329 AM CST Fri Nov 27 2026\n\nAn upper level ridge will build over the region on Saturday.\nHighs will warm into the 50s across much of the area with some\nlow 60 readings across portions of far southern Missouri \nSaturday afternoon. \n\nAn upper level trough will move into the central Plains on\nSaturday. Surface low pressure will across of Oklahoma will lift\nnortheast across the area Saturday night into Sunday ahead of\nthe trough. Moisture return will be limited ahead of this \nas surface high pressure will be in place south of the area \nahead of the system. A band of rain is expected to develop \nnorthwest of the surface low and ahead of the upper level \ntrough. As the low lifts Northeast across a cold front will be \neast across the area Saturday night into Sunday morning. A \nwarmer air mass will be in place as temperatures remain in the \n40s ahead of the cold front Saturday night. Lows will likely \noccur behind the cold front Sunday morning. Current rain chances\nfor this system range from 40 to 80% across the area with the \nbetter chances occurring with the band of rain north of the low,\nwhich will be north of I-44 and especially north of Hwy 54. A\nlittle shift to the north or south of the track of the surface\nlow would also shift this band of rain with it some. \n\nFurther to the south the rain chances will occur along the cold\nfront and will be short lived in nature as the front will \nquickly move east across the area. Coverage may also be more \nscattered in nature along the front and all locations may not \nsee rain across southern Missouri due to the limited moisture \nreturn, especially further to the south and west. Coverage could\nincrease some across south central Missouri as a little better\nmoisture may be able to return as the front will push through\nthese locations later. Some weak instability will also develop \nacross portions of south central Missouri ahead of the front \nand there is a low chance (<20%) for an isolated thunderstorm \ndeveloping. Rainfall amounts will overall be on the light side \ngenerally up to one to two tenths of an inch expected.\n\nThe pressure gradient will tighten across the area behind the \narea of surface low pressure Sunday, resulting in gusty \nnorthwesterly winds occurring. Gusts up to 25 to 35 mph will \noccur at times on Sunday. Cooler temperatures will also move \ninto the area behind the front with highs in the 40s on Sunday\nas lows Sunday night into Monday morning drop into the 20s.\n\nHighs will warm into the 50s early next week, as another upper\nlevel short wave trough moves through region. A dry air mass \nwill be place so not precipitation is expected with the trough. \nAn upper level ridge then builds over the region by the middle \nof next week with highs warming well into the 60s by the \nWednesday. There are some model members with readings in the \nlower 70s for some locations.\n\nAn upper level low will move on to the west coast by the middle\nof next week and the ensemble model members indicate it will \nmove to the northeast and across the region late next week. \nThere are differences on the track and timing of the system with\nbetween the model members.",
